  "summary": "Rhinestones, velour tracksuits and flip phones: the 2000s in one outfit.",
  "description": "McBling (term coined by Tavi Gevinson-era writers, canonized by fashion historians like Liana Satenstein) names the 2003-2008 explosion of logo glam: Juicy velour tracksuits, rhinestone-studded everything, Von Dutch trucker hats, UGGs with miniskirts, flip phones with charms, and Paris-Hilton-era paparazzi flash. Descended from hip-hop bling and tabloid celebrity culture, it was maximal, expensive-looking and unashamed; MySpace glamor shots were its native format. The 2010s rejected it as \"trashy\", and the 2020s Y2K revival re-adopted it ironically-then-sincerely. Its visual signature: flash-photography gloss, brand-as-costume, sparkle against velour.",
  "culturalContext": "McBling named the 2000s' rhinestone maximalism: velour, Von Dutch and flip-phone glam defined celebrity culture's flash decade, the 2010s rejected it as trashy, and the Y2K revival re-adopted it sincerely — the era now reads as pop history's most honest consumer optimism.",
